course: everyday
lesson: 81
title: Food, drinks and feeling ill
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: What you eat and drink is fuel for your body: water keeps every part working, and fruit, yoghurt or nuts between meals keep your energy steady instead of up and down.
explain: Some foods are dangerous for some people: a person with an allergy can become very ill from a tiny amount, and small round foods like whole grapes can block a small child's airway.
explain: When you are ill, your body is busy fighting germs; it needs rest, fluids and time, and you stay home so you do not pass the germs on.
explain: A girl in Manchester wakes up hot and shivery; she tells her mum, stays in bed with a drink of water, and is back at school after her fever has been gone for a day.
fact: Share food with a friend only if neither of you has an allergy and an adult agrees.
fact: A healthy snack between meals, like fruit, yoghurt or nuts if you are not allergic, keeps your energy steady.
fact: A person who is choking but can still cough should keep coughing, because coughing is the body's own way of pushing the food out; if they cannot breathe, speak or cough, shout for an adult and call the emergency number.
fact: Grapes should be cut lengthways for small children, because small round food can block a small airway.
fact: You can swim after a normal meal; there is no good evidence that swimming after eating causes drowning.
fact: Children need about six to eight cups of water a day, and more when it is hot or they are active.
fact: Water and milk are the best drinks; juice is very sugary, so have only a small glass once a day.
fact: Alcohol, coffee and energy drinks are not for children, because they can harm a growing body.
fact: With a fever, tell a parent, rest and drink plenty; stay off school until you have had no fever for at least a day.
fact: After being sick or having diarrhoea, stay home and sip water; in the UK children stay off school until 48 hours after the last time.
fact: A rash that does not fade when you press a glass on it needs urgent medical help, because it can be a sign of meningitis, a very dangerous illness.
fact: Most sore throats get better by themselves in a few days with drinks and rest.
fact: Scratching chickenpox spots can cause infection and scars, so keep nails short and use the cooling lotion an adult gives you.
exercise: A friend at school offers you half of her sandwich. When is it fine to share? | When neither of you has an allergy and an adult agrees | Whenever she offers it kindly, because sharing is always good | Only if you swap something back | Only if the sandwich has no bread
exercise: You are hungry an hour before dinner. Which snack is the best choice? | An apple or a small pot of yoghurt | A big bag of sweets to share | A can of energy drink and some crisps | Nothing at all until tomorrow
exercise: Your friend is choking but coughing hard. What should happen? | Let them keep coughing and get an adult ready | Give them a big glass of water to drink at once | Tell them to lie down flat | Make them eat some bread
exercise: Your friend is choking and cannot cough, breathe or speak. What do you do? | Shout for an adult and call the emergency number | Wait quietly beside them for it to pass by itself | Give them a drink to wash it down | Tell them to run around the room
exercise: Why are grapes cut lengthways for little children? | Small round food can block a small child's airway | Grapes taste much sweeter when they are cut in half that way | Whole grapes are poisonous for babies | It makes the grapes last longer
exercise: Which is true about swimming after eating? | You can swim after a normal meal | You must always wait a full hour | You will get a cramp and drown | Swimming after lunch is against the law
exercise: About how much water does a child need each day? | About six to eight cups, more when hot | About one small cup a day | About thirty cups a day | None at all if they eat plenty of fruit
exercise: Why is it better to drink water than juice with every meal? | Juice has a lot of sugar in it | Juice has no vitamins in it at all | Water has more energy in it than juice | Juice makes you more thirsty
exercise: Why are energy drinks and coffee not for children? | They can harm a growing body | They only taste nice to adults | They make children grow too fast | They are too expensive for children
exercise: You wake up hot and achy with a fever. What should you do? | Tell a parent, rest and drink fluids | Go for a long run to sweat it out | Hide it from everyone and go to school | Take any pills you find
exercise: You were sick in the night. What is the right thing to do? | Stay home and keep sipping water | Go to school as long as you feel fine | Eat a big breakfast to get strong | Go swimming to feel fresh
exercise: Why should you stay home with a high temperature? | You need rest and could make others ill | Teachers do not like hot children in class | It is only a rule in winter | A fever is caught from the weather and the cold
exercise: You feel a bit ill but your friends are playing football. What is best? | Rest quietly so your body can fight the illness | Play extra hard to shake the illness off and sweat it out | Play but drink an energy drink | Go out but do not tell anyone
exercise: A purple rash does not fade when a glass is pressed on it. What should happen? | Tell an adult at once; it needs urgent help | Put some cream on it and wait a week or so | Ignore it if it does not itch | Wash it with cold water and forget about it
exercise: What usually helps a sore throat? | Warm or cool drinks, rest and time | Shouting a lot to stretch the throat | Eating crisps to scratch it | Staying outside in the cold air all day
exercise: Your chickenpox spots itch a lot. What should you do? | Try not to scratch and use cooling lotion | Scratch them off so they heal faster and go away | Pop them all with a pin | Cover them with sticky tape
test: A boy in Sydney sees his baby cousin about to be given whole grapes. What could he tell an adult? | Small children's grapes should be cut lengthways | Babies should have the grapes frozen hard first | Grapes should be peeled but left whole so they go down | Grapes are fine if the baby is sitting
test: Which is the best everyday drink for a thirsty child after football? | Water | An energy drink | A large glass of cola | A cup of strong coffee
test: Why does the advice say to keep coughing when food goes down the wrong way? | Coughing is the body's own way of pushing food out | Coughing makes the food smaller | Coughing calls the food back up to the mouth to be chewed again | Coughing warms the throat so food melts
test: A girl in Toronto has had no fever since yesterday morning and feels well. What is true? | She can probably go back to school | She must stay home for another month | She must still take fever medicine | She should go to hospital to be checked
test: Why do children in the UK stay off school for 48 hours after a tummy bug? | Tummy bugs spread very easily to others | Teachers need time to clean all the desks and chairs | The tummy is too weak to sit up | It is a rule about school dinners
test: A friend says, «Feed yourself juice all day when you are thirsty.» What is the problem? | Juice has a lot of sugar, so water is better | Juice has too little sugar in it to give any help | Juice makes you lose all your water | Juice stops your body from growing
test: What would be a worrying sign that needs urgent medical help? | A rash that does not fade under a pressed glass | A sore throat on the first day | Feeling a little tired after school | Sneezing a few times in spring
test: Why is resting important when you are ill? | The body needs its energy to fight the infection | Germs only die when you lie flat | Moving around makes you catch a new illness on top | Sleeping makes the germs fall asleep too
test: A boy in Florida finishes a light lunch and wants to swim. What is true? | He can swim; waiting an hour is a myth | He must wait exactly one hour after eating | He will surely get a cramp and sink | He may only paddle until dinner
test: Why should you not scratch chickenpox? | Scratching can cause infection and scars | Scratching spreads the spots to your hair | Scratching makes the illness last a year | Scratching turns the spots into warts
test: nan says no swimming for an hour after lunch or ull drown | No, you can swim after a normal meal | Yes, swimming after eating causes drowning | Yes, wait at least two hours | Yes, but only after a big lunch
test: hes coughing loads on his crisp, cant talk tho now, cant breathe | Shout for an adult and call the emergency number | Give him water to wash it down quick | Let him lie down and rest | Wait, he will cough it up soon, just give it a minute
test: pressed a glass on her rash and it didnt fade | Get urgent medical help, it can be meningitis | That is normal, rashes never fade under glass | Put cream on and wait a week | Wash it and go to school
test: fever went last night, back to school today?? | No, wait till no fever for at least a day | Yes, the fever has gone now so you're fine | Yes, if you feel a bit better | Yes, if you take medicine first
explain: A boy in Arizona played football all afternoon in the heat without drinking; he got a headache, felt dizzy and had to be cooled down by the coach.
explain: A girl in Leeds ate chicken that had been left out on the counter all day; she was sick with food poisoning for two days.
fact: In hot weather children can become dehydrated quickly, with headache, tiredness and dizziness as the first signs; then you stop, sit in the shade, drink water and tell an adult.
fact: When parents are «splitting up», they are separating or divorcing, and a child can feel ill from the worry.
fact: Someone who says they are «lovesick» means they feel so sad or excited about a person they like that they do not feel like eating; it is not a real illness.
fact: Cooked food left out at room temperature for hours can grow germs that cause food poisoning.
test: hot day, been playing ages, head hurts n dizzy | Stop, sit in the shade, drink water and tell an adult | Keep playing, it goes away | Drink a fizzy drink and run about some more | Lie in the sun to warm up
test: pizza been on the table since last night, can i eat it | No, food left out for hours can make you sick | Yes, cooked food never goes bad | Yes, if you blow on it first | Only if it still smells fine
test: feel really sick and been throwing up all day | Tell an adult, sip water, they may call a doctor | Hide it and go to school | Eat lots of crisps to settle it | Drink nothing so you don't throw up
test: my mum's mate said a kid at football collapsed in the heat cos he didn't drink all day, what's the real lesson | Drink plenty of water, more when it is hot or you are active | Never play football in summer at all | Drink only energy drinks when it is hot | Heat only affects adults, not kids
test: kids reckon if u swim straight after lunch u get a cramp and drown for sure, true? | A myth; there is no good evidence it causes drowning | True, you must wait two full hours | True, but only after a big pudding | True, so nobody swims on school days
test: everyone's saying Noah's sick cos his family's food is dirty, should i spread it | No, it's unkind and you do not know why he is ill | Yes, everyone should avoid eating at his house | Yes, but only tell the people he invites over | Yes, and say his mum cannot cook
exam: Identify the conduct appropriate to a person choking who remains able to cough. | The person should continue to cough | The person should lie down and remain silent | The person should drink a large glass of water | The person should be struck hard on the head
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a rash that does not fade beneath a glass pressed upon it? | It requires urgent medical help, as it may signify meningitis | It is a minor rash that will fade by morning | It is caused by the glass and requires nothing | It indicates a mild allergy to the drinking glass
exam: Determine, pursuant to the established guidance, when a child who has had a fever may return to school. | When there has been no fever for at least a day | Immediately after taking a dose of medicine | When the fever is at its very highest | One week after the fever first began
exam: Identify the principal reason for which grapes are cut lengthways for small children. | Small round food may block a small airway | Whole grapes are too sour for small children | Cut grapes contain fewer germs than whole ones | Small children cannot digest grape skins
test: teacher left early n kids saying "she's having a baby, she's been sick every morning" | That is a guess about her private life; do not spread it, people feel ill for many reasons | It is certain, so tell the whole school | Being sick always means a baby | Ask her about it loudly next lesson
test: Sam's off school n someone said "his mum n dad are splitting up, he's sick from it" | Sam's parents may be divorcing and he may feel unwell from worry; do not spread it, just be kind when he is back | It means Sam ate bad food | It means Sam caused it | Tell everyone so they ask Sam about it
test: big sis says "i'm lovesick" n won't eat her tea lol | She means she is so sad or excited about someone she likes that she does not feel hungry; it is not an illness, so be kind | She has a germ called love, so call the doctor | She ate too many sweets | It means she is lying, so tell mum
